problem:
With r ≈ 0.984, little throughput is lost at B itself. Most of the delay that preventing breakdown could save must then come from the queue's spillback:
- blocked exits inside the queue (Pioneer Tr 5.818, Anderson Lakes Pkwy 3.751);
- held-back upstream merges (I-494 at 1.73–1.81).

P1–P6 test none of this:
- propagation is only 'reported';
- exit flows are never compared;
- the maximum extent of the queue is not checked.

The realism gate's R-b (wave or propagation speed) has also been dropped, although METANET was chosen over CTM precisely for its speed dynamics. A plant can pass P1–P6 while getting wrong the very mechanism that carries any headroom.

F1 is not specified enough to be pre-registered. The protocol does not say:
- which METANET quantity switches;
- what triggers the memory switch and what releases it;
- how many cells it covers;
- how many extra parameters it adds, and their bounds;
- what 'per-cell v_f for the merge cells' means (there are about 20 entrances).
F1 can only be used after P1-P6 have failed on validation days. Leaving these choices open lets validation results shape the fallback design, which is a forking path. The cited source is also a first-order (LWR/Godunov) model, not a METANET extension, so the adaptation must be written down and disclosed.

